# Project

Lennar interview code challenge: a React web app that implements a responsive hero section from Figma — navbar with mobile menu drawer + overlay, callout pill, display heading, email signup form, and illustration — across four breakpoints.

- Source of truth for every visual value: @docs/design-spec.md
- Build order and ownership split: @docs/BUILD_ORDER.md
- Base UI components (buttons, typography, inputs) are built in-house. Never use UI libraries: no MUI, Bootstrap, Chakra, shadcn/ui, Headless UI, Radix, Mantine, Ant.
- Reviewers will read every file and ask the author architectural questions. Write code a senior engineer can defend line by line. Prefer the boring, obvious solution.

# Stack (do not change without asking)

- Vite + React 19 + TypeScript (strict)
- Tailwind CSS v4 via `@tailwindcss/vite`; design tokens in `src/styles/index.css` under `@theme`
- Inter, self-hosted via `@fontsource-variable/inter`
- Vitest + `@testing-library/react` + `@testing-library/user-event` + `@testing-library/jest-dom` + jsdom
- ESLint flat config (`typescript-eslint` strictTypeChecked + stylisticTypeChecked, `eslint-plugin-react-hooks`, `eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y`) + Prettier
- GraphQL bonus uses plain `fetch`. No Apollo, urql, graphql-request, axios.
- Never add a dependency without asking first. When proposing one, state its gzip size and why the platform or React cannot do the job.

# Scripts (create these in package.json during scaffold)

- `dev`, `build`, `preview`
- `typecheck` → `tsc -b`
- `lint` → `eslint .` ; `format` → `prettier --write .`
- `test` → all tests ; `test:agent` → excludes `**/*.spec.ts(x)` (human-owned acceptance specs)
- `check` → typecheck + lint + test + build
- `check:agent` → typecheck + lint + test:agent + build

# Workflow

1. Before writing code, state a short plan: files to create or change and why.
2. Make small, focused changes — one concern at a time.
3. After every change run `npm run check:agent`. Do not report a task as done until it is green.
4. Never weaken a tsconfig flag, lint rule, or test to get green. Fix the code instead.
5. Respect the human-owned boundary (see `80-human-owned.mdc`). If a task needs a human-owned module that is still a stub, stop and say which file blocks you.
6. If the design spec is ambiguous or silent, ask. Do not invent design.
7. Do not commit or push unless explicitly asked. Propose a commit message instead.

# Comments policy

- No inline or block comments that explain code. Names and structure must explain it.
- JSDoc is required on every exported function, hook, component, class and public method: a one-line summary, plus `@param`/`@returns` only when the types don't already make it obvious.
- No commented-out code, no TODO/FIXME (the only exception is the stub marker in human-owned files), no banner or section comments.
- No `eslint-disable` comments of any kind.
